A||||13||drednot||A.C. Help, This may stump the best of you!||||11||
Z||000000||drednot||04-09-2001||06:24 PM||||I just got the new retrofit fit kit for the R-135A.  I charged up the system like the directions say.  I am right in the green on the gauge.  System holding presure good.  After charging, the compressor still would not kick on.    First thing I checked is the compressor.  it is not ceased.  I looked at the fuses in the car, all fine.  I checked the wirers on the back if the compressor, both clean and conected.  I checked the conections to the beer-can looking thing under the hood.  They are clean/conected.  As you can probably tell from reading this, A.C. systems are not my area of experteese, but I am fairly sharp when it comes to cars, and I would like to fix this one myself.  I am sure I missed a few things that I should check, it would be a big help if you all could let me know what I missed.  One thing that would help me is I would like to know Is how to bypass the system controls and activate the compressor clutch so I can see if the clutch is even working, and if the system circulates and cools.  If the clutch is working and circulating the system, that should narrow it down for me.  Thanks for any info that you may have. <P> P.S.  Got my suspension fixed.  works great now.    ||||reg||11
Z||000001||Taijiguy||04-09-2001||07:00 PM||||Well, I'm not real fluent in A/C either, but I know there's a relay that controls the compressor, it's one of those over on the drivers side firewall. It gets power from the ECM (if my Haynes is correct) Might check to see if that relay is closing, and for the presence of power on the contacts.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000002||operator64||04-09-2001||08:44 PM||||   There is a preassure switch on the beer can looking thing ( accumulator ) there should be 2 wires running to it. If you cross these your compressor should turn on.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by operator64 (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-09-2001||08:54 PM||||If you used one of those kits they sell in auto parts stores, you may have killed it. First, the oil in those kits is bad for the desicant in 80's GM vehicles. It turns the desicant to goo. Or if the system was not a sealed unit prior to converting over, the desicant could also be toast. This can block the system. The compressor won't kick on unless pressure is present. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||04-09-2001||09:23 PM||||You should look at the connectors at the A/C compressor. There is on in the front, just behind the pulley pointing toward the top of the passenger seat. This controls the clutch of the compressor and causes it to engage.<BR>The are two connectors on the back side facing toward the starter. One is the LOW pressure switch (connector on the compressor is green I think). The other is red indicating HIGH pressure. The pulley on the compressor is 'free wheeling' so I need to ask how you determined that it is not seized (locked-up).<BR>I need to say this as well, You cannot charge the system with the compressor not working correctly. What directions did you follow and what steps did you take when charging? It is very possible that you created a HIGH PRESSURE condition on the LOW side lines. If you did, and every thing does work the way that it should, The compressor will not engage if this condition is present.<BR>Please clarify how you charged the system as there may be a way to get it to work without discharging the system.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||drednot||04-09-2001||11:36 PM||||The thing I just tried was crossing the 2 wires running to the accumulator.  The compressor did not kick on.  Something did however spark, and start to smell like burnt electronics.  I checked to see if the compressor was ceased by spinning the part that sticks out from the fly wheel of the compressor.   In other words, I spun (with a scrue driver) the part that the compressor is supposed to start spinning when the clutch is engaged (when it is working of course).  The directions said to fill up the low side with oil/freon while the car is running with a.c. on full blast.  I filled it to the almost full point on the gauge.  At that point-- Car on, A.C. on, system full, compressor not cycling.  I am stumped.  What I really need is a step by stem list of the things that I need to check to get the system working.  I looked in my manual, but they basically don't tell you anything about the A.C. system except how to change major components, which I don't need to do.  It is getting hot around here, and this car is black and has no A.C.  My other car I had before the fiero, I used a similar kit on with great results, It worked for 2 years, and I used it a lot.  but this has me bumed.  I would hate to have to go to the Pontiac dealer and have them fix it.  Heck, it would probably cost more than the car is worth for that.  Please help if you can.  Thanks a lot.   <p>[This message has been edited by drednot (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000006||drednot||04-10-2001||07:51 AM||||the spark and burning smell was when I conected the 2 wirers on the accumilator to ground.  I am sure I burnt something out, but I don't know what.||||reg||11
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||04-10-2001||08:27 AM||||I'll do some checking when I can to see what the may have done. Have you checked your fuses to see if one popped after that?||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||04-10-2001||01:28 PM||||My recollections from my own shade tree AC experiences...<P>You should have connected the wires to each other... not to ground. You probably blew a fuse or maybe a fusable link. I believe that switch is a low pressure switch that keeps the compressor from running when the system is discharged... If so, you usually have to jumper them to get the compressor to run in a discharged system long enough to build the charge up.||||reg||1
Z||000009||drednot||04-10-2001||08:30 PM||||Ok, This is starting to get me mad.  The first thing I looked at was my fuse panel.  no blown fuses.  what next?||||reg||8
Z||000010||Turbo1dr||04-10-2001||10:54 PM||||What year and what engine? Need to know that for proper wiring diagnosis.<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo1dr (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000011||drednot||04-11-2001||09:32 AM||||it is a 1985 4 Cyl.  ||||reg||3
Z||000012||drednot||04-11-2001||04:52 PM||||Ok, I am at the point where I just want to see if the system will work!  How do I bypass everything and get the compressor clutch to come on.  The compressor has 2 wires running into it.  what do I do to get it to turn on?  I am considering running a switch from my dash to the compressor clutch so I can cycle it on and off manually.  thoughts?  suggestions??||||reg||8
Z||000013||Turbo1dr||04-11-2001||09:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>Ok, I am at the point where I just want to see if the system will work!  How do I bypass everything and get the compressor clutch to come on.  The compressor has 2 wires running into it.  what do I do to get it to turn on?  I am considering running a switch from my dash to the compressor clutch so I can cycle it on and off manually.  thoughts?  suggestions??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes. That will let you know if the system will cool or not.  But be sure to have your gauges hooked up to the system so you can see the pressures. You don't want to operate an A/C system that may have a restriction somewhere. Too much pressure will damage it.<P><BR>If the system has pressure (more than 30psi on the low side) do the following simple test:<P>With everything hooked up properly start the engine and turn the A/C on.  With a test light see if you are getting any voltage to the compressor clutch coil at the connector.  If you <I>are</I> and it's still not engaging it could be the clutch plate isn't close enough to the pulley for the magnet to pull it towards it.  You can tap on the outer plate to help it engage sometimes. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
